A||||3||Carrolles||Cruise Control Vacuum Source ?||||1||
Z||000000||Carrolles||12-09-1999||07:23 AM||||I'm about finished with my new motor installation and will move on to installing my new JC Whitney cruise control unit.<P>Where does the original cruise control in the v'6 cars get its vacuum?  The instructions caution me not to splice into the power brake vacuum line.||||reg||1
Z||000001||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||12:05 PM||||I'm not to sure about the V-6, but the 4 cyl have a vacuum fitting on the intake that has 3 different lines to it.  One of these runs the cruise.  There should be a place where the EGR gets its vacuum from, check there, or you may have to buy a different fitting that has a place for your cruise.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||12-09-1999||12:48 PM||||Carroll, I just went and looked. Just under the map sensor are two rubber lines going toward the trunk. There they connect to two steel lines that run across the engine compartment on the back side wall to the evaporation canister.<BR>The vacume comes off of one of these steel lines. I have an 86 SE without cruise that has the connecton T capped off. Look at the rear left corner of the engine compartment where it makes the turn toward the canister. It has a rubber cap on the fitting. <BR> If I recall, this is the same car you have.<BR>Coy<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-09-1999||02:22 PM||||I do have that same capped off connector and was hoping that is what it is for.<P>Thanks||||reg||1
